    { "answer": ["Alcohol withdrawal","Delirium tremens","DT","DTs"], "clues": ["In 'The Adventures of Huckleberry Finn', Huck's father suffers from this condition where he thought snakes were biting his legs. If that's not helpful, Nicolas Cage also played a character who  in the 1995 film, Leaving Las Vegas. His performance won him the Academy Award for Best Actor in 1996","For centuries, this was misidentified as 'phrenitis' or an inflammation of the brain. It was formally identified and named in 1813, which marked a turning point in the clinical recognition of this as a disease that could be managed medically","Patients might develop confusion, shaking, and vivid hallucinations which popularized an older idiom 'seeing pink elephants'"] }
{ "answer": ["breast cancer","breast CA","breast adenocarcinoma","breast adeno"], "clues": ["Around 3000-2500BCE, this was described in the Edwin Smith Papyrus, an ancient Egyptian medical text, where several cases were mentioned as an incurable condition, suggesting treatment via cauterization with a tool called the 'fire drill'. Other historical treatments included bloodletting or even placing raw veal on ulcerated tissue based on the belief that it would feed internal 'worms' devouring the flesh.","Historically, this was sometimes called the 'nun's disease' because it was observed to affected nuns at higher rates than other populations. It was later confirmed that lifelong celibacy increases the risk of this disease.","In the 1990s, the pink ribbon became a global symbol for awareness and advocacy for this disease, sparking public education and leaps in research funding"] },
{ "answer": ["Cirrhosis"], "clues": ["In 1947, the 'Four Brothers' was a famous jazz tune that became a jazz standard featuring three tenor saxophones and one baritone saxophone. Unfortunately, all four saxophonists who played in the original lineup later developed this disease, which was .","The name of this condition was coined in 1819 by Rene Laennec, who is more famously known for inventing the stethoscope. He derived it from the Greek word meaning 'orange-yellow' describing the color of the diseased organ","During the American Prohibition, mortality rates of this disease dropped by more than 50%. In modern times, viral causes remain common in many parts of the world although incidence in the Americas is dropping with an increase in metabolic and alcohol related cases."] },
{ "answer": ["Crohn's disease","Crohn's","crohns disease","crohns","IBD","Inflammatory bowel disease"], "clues": ["President Eisenhower suffered from years of abdominal pain and was diagnosed with this disease first by an x-ray series and then later confirmed when he underwent emergency surgery for a small bowel obstruction. He recovered and won his re-election as president of the United States the next year.","King Alfred the Great (849-900AD) is also thought to have suffered from this disease with painful debilitating episodes of abdominal pain, peri-anal lesions, and diarrhea","The first recorded description of this disease may have been by Greek physician Aretaeus of Cappadocia in the 1st century although it was not formally identified and named until 1932 when an American Gastroenterologist published a landmark paper describing a disease of the terminal ileum affecting mainly young adults"] },
{ "answer": ["SGLT2 inhibitors","SGLT2"], "clues": ["In 1835, French and Belgian chemists isolated phlorizin from the root bark of apple trees, which blocked transporters in the intestines and kidneys leading to severe diarrhea, cramping, and malabsorption. Switching from an oxygen atom to a carbon atom allowed for this more selective class of medications to be developed.","These medications block sodium and glucose reabsorption in the proximal tubule which leads to constriction of the afferent arteriole, reducing stress on the glomeruli and slowing the progression of chronic kidney disease","This class of medications can also reduce heart failure hospitalizations but the glucosuria can cause euglycemic DKA and increase the risk of UTIs."] },
{ "answer": ["Montelukast","Singulair","leukotriene inhibitor"], "clues": ["This medication was discovered and developed at the Merck Frosst research hub located in a suburb of Montreal, Canada. By the time its patent expired in 2012, this was Merck's top-selling product, generating roughly $5 billion a year in sales","Part of the name of this medication is actually from the location it was discovered. Although this medication was approved by the FDA in 1998, after years of consumer advocacy and post-market tracking, the FDA attached a Black Box Warning in 2020 to notify patients of potential neuropsychiatric risks including vivid nightmares, depression, and suicidal thoughts.","In 1930, researchers found an unknown substance in the sputum of asthmatics and named it the 'Slow-Reacting Substance of Anaphylaxis'. It wasn't until the 70s and 80s that scienties figured out these were a group of inflammatory chemicals called leukotrienes. This medication was the first highly effective drug to block those molecules."] },
{ "answer": ["Pertussis","whooping cough"], "clues": ["The first descriptions of this disease came from Guillaume de Baillou in 1578 where he described: 'symptoms are severe. The lung is so irritated that, at every attempt to expel the cause of his distress, he can neither inhale nor exhale. The sufferer seems to swell up and, as if about to strangle himself, holds his breath'","In the 1930s in Michigan, two female scientists, Pearl Kendrick and Grace Eldering, started research into this disease in their free time after work. They mixed their own specialized agar plates and asked sick children to cough on each plate to collect the causal bacteria, which allowed them to develop a potent whole-cell vaccine","Some individuals affected by this disease develop a characteristic high-pitched gasping sound after a coughing fit, but babies might become apneic instead. The vaccine is recommended starting from the age of 2 months and protects against this disease as well as two others."] },
{ "answer": ["Artemisinin","artesunate"], "clues": ["This medication is sometimes described as 'promiscuous' as it binds to many targets to cause cell death. Iron-mediated cleavage forms reactive carbon radicals that bind to and destroy vital proteins and cell membranes.","In the late 1960s, soldiers in the Vietnam War suffered greatly from drug-resistant malaria. The Vietnamese army asked China for help and the Chinese government started a classified military operation to find a new cure. Tu Youyou found a 4th century text that recommended soaking sweet wormwood in cold water to treat intermittent fevers which led to the discovery of this medication","This medication remains part of the global standard first-line treatment for malaria in ACTs. It is the 'A' in that acronym."] },
